  \uses{def:pic_et_sheaf}
  By construction: \cref{def:pic_et_sheaf} is a sheafification. The set-valued
  claim follows because the forgetful functor \(\Ab \to \Set\) preserves the
  limits the sheaf condition is expressed by.

  This lemma is what makes \cref{thm:fga_pic_representability_etale} a
  \emph{consistent} statement rather than a false one: a representable functor
  is a sheaf for any subcanonical topology, so the corresponding assertion for
  the unsheafified \(\Pic^\sharp_{C/k}\) --- which is not a sheaf --- could not
  hold with no hypothesis on \(C(k)\).
\end{proof}

